3. Amagi

Amagi CONNECT

Amagi is especially interesting if you want to build a large streaming/FAST service rather than a traditional satellite TV service.

Its CONNECT marketplace connects content owners and platforms. Amagi currently says its marketplace has 400+ channels and 50+ platform profiles and allows platforms to discover content providers and negotiate distribution arrangements.

That is much closer to the type of infrastructure you are building for BTECH-TV.
4. MultiChoice

MultiChoice

MultiChoice operates the DStv/GOtv ecosystem and licenses/creates local and international programming for satellite, terrestrial and OTT distribution.

However, you should not expect to simply buy a DStv playlist or DStv subscription and redistribute its channels. Commercial redistribution requires the appropriate rights and agreements.

DStv's current Nigerian offering itself contains more than 100 channels across sports, movies, entertainment, kids, documentary, news and other categories.
What I would recommend for your BTECH-TV model

Instead of trying to obtain "14,000 DStv channels," structure BTECH-TV as a licensed aggregation platform:

BTECH-TV
→ Licensed African channels
→ Licensed international channels
→ Licensed FAST channels
→ Licensed movie channels
→ Licensed news channels
→ Licensed sports channels
→ Free-to-air channels where redistribution is permitted
→ Your own BTECH-TV channels
→ VOD/movie catalogue with appropriate rights

You can then potentially reach hundreds or thousands of legitimate channels by combining multiple licensors, rather than trying to obtain everything from one company.

Also, be careful with the IPTV lists you've been working with: a URL working in VLC or an M3U playlist does not establish that you have the right to redistribute that channel commercially.

